RailMind is more feasible. Here's why:
Scoping clarity. RailMind has a single, linear narrative — sensor in → agents process → resolution out. Each of the 5 agents has a well-defined, non-overlapping job. You can build them sequentially and the demo works even if one agent is simplified. ExamShield v2 has three interlocking systems (behavioral fingerprinting + dynamic question generation + IRT adaptive engine) that all need to work together to tell a coherent story.
The hard technical risk. ExamShield's core differentiator — the behavioral fingerprint — requires collecting enough keystroke/mouse data during a "practice session" to build a meaningful model, then detecting a deviation when Student B sits down. In a hackathon demo environment with a fresh setup, this is fragile. If the fingerprint doesn't clearly flag the impersonation, the whole demo collapses. RailMind's demo works on purely simulated/synthetic data you control end-to-end.
Division of work. With 2 people, RailMind splits cleanly: one person owns the agent backend (LangGraph, agent logic, FastAPI), the other owns the frontend (Next.js dashboard, WebSocket streaming, simulated sensor feed). ExamShield doesn't split as cleanly — the WASM keystroke module, the ML classifier, the IRT engine, and the LLM question generator all require different expertise and touch the same demo flow.
Dependencies. RailMind's only external dependency is an LLM API and ChromaDB — both trivial to set up. ExamShield needs a working scikit-learn/PyTorch behavioral model, a WASM build pipeline, and a convincing LLM question generator that produces valid exam questions. Each is a potential day-eating rabbit hole.
The one thing RailMind demands: the dashboard needs to look polished, not like a terminal printout. Budget real time for the frontend — visible agent reasoning chains are the entire "wow" moment.
Bottom line: ExamShield v2 is the more innovative idea on paper, but for a 2-person team in ~6 days, RailMind gives you a complete, reliable, impressive demo with far less integration risk. Build RailMind.

